        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Merach treadmills are reasonable value for walking and light jogging, and the NovaWalk W50 TrekPad is
            the one to buy. They are not running treadmills: no current Merach goes faster than 7.5 mph, and the warranty
            is one year, or two years if you register on Merach's site.</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Walking pads:</strong> the W50 TrekPad and W60 Plus, both $299.99 on Merach's store, 0.6 to 4 mph
            with powered incline (checked November 2026).</li>
            <li><strong>Folding treadmills:</strong> four T12 versions from $369.99 to $599.99, 0.5 to 7.5 mph, three of
            them with 15% powered incline.</li>
            <li><strong>Capacity:</strong> 300 lb on most T12s, 320 lb on the T12 Pro, and up to 400 lb on the W50
            TrekPad.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty:</strong> one year on the frame and parts, plus a second year for registered members,
            according to Merach's warranty page.</li>
            <li><strong>Returns:</strong> 30 days on Merach's own store, in resellable condition and <a href="/how-to-pack-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>original packaging</a>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Merach is one of several Amazon-era brands that publish more detail than most, including continuous motor
            ratings. It also publishes figures that contradict each other on the same page, so the comparison below
            flags every conflict. If you came here from our guide to <a href="/lifesmart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LifeSmart treadmills</a>, the difference
            in one line: LifeSmart's best-known machines are full-size self-folding treadmills at about $1,000 and up,
            while Merach sells compact walking machines under $600. Nothing on this page was tested hands-on; it is built from Merach's store, retailer
            listings and independent coverage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-is-merach" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Makes Merach Treadmills?</h2>
<p>
            Merach's About page says the company was "Founded in 2018 in Hangzhou, China", and the footer of its US store
            gives a registered address on Weiye Road in Hangzhou (checked November 2026). A business database lists the
            company as MERACH (Hangzhou) Health Technology Co., Ltd. It sells under the Merach name, with stores at
            merachfit.com for the United States and separate storefronts for the UK, Germany and Australia.
          </p>
          <p>
            Treadmills are a small part of what it makes. On 21 November 2026 its US walking-pad and treadmill
            collection listed six machines, beside eleven rowing machines, eight exercise bikes, two vibration plates, a <a href="/best-smith-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Smith machine</a>, benches, steppers and accessories. Every treadmill and pad on the list connects to the Merach
            app for iOS and Android, according to its product pages.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where Merach is sold</h3>
          <p>
            In the US, Merach sells through its own store, Amazon, and marketplace listings <a href="/best-buy-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>at Best Buy</a>, Walmart and
            Newegg. Those channels do not always carry the same models. The W50 Lite, for example, appears on Amazon and
            Best Buy but not on Merach's US store, and older models such as the T19 2-in-1 have been reported as
            Amazon-only by a walking-pad comparison site. Where you buy matters for two reasons covered further down: Merach's 30-day return policy only
            covers its own store, and its warranty extension depends on registering with Merach.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Naming: NovaWalk, UltraWalk, TrekPad and T12</h3>
          <p>
            Merach's names stack a series (NovaWalk, UltraWalk), a model (W50, W60 Plus) and a nickname (TrekPad). The
            store SKUs are more reliable: MR-T25B2-US is the W50 TrekPad, MR-T27S2-US and MR-T27B2A-US are the W60 Plus in
            two colours, and the four T12s run MR-T12B2-US (base), MR-T12B3-US (Aura), MR-T12B4-US (Pro) and
            MR-T12B5-US (with handles). A Best Buy listing uses MR-T25B4 for the W50 Lite, which shows how easily the
            W50 and W50 Lite get mixed up.
          </p>
        </div>

