          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where the figures disagree</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>W60 Plus capacity:</strong> Merach's page says 330 lb; Garage Gym Reviews and a walking-pad
            comparison site say 400 lb. Use 330 lb until Merach's page and box agree on something higher.</li>
            <li><strong>W50 TrekPad incline:</strong> Merach says up to 12%; some retailer titles and a published
            roundup say 10%. Several Best Buy titles also attach 350 lb and 3 HP to the "W50" name, figures that belong
            to the W50 Lite.</li>
            <li><strong>T12 Pro belt:</strong> 17.7 by 47.3 inches in the spec table and 43.3 by 17.3 inches in the
            feature text, on the same page.</li>
            <li><strong>T12 Aura:</strong> 320 lb, a 17.7 by 47.3-inch belt and 3.0 HP peak in the spec table; 300 lb, a
            42.5 by 16.5-inch belt and 2.5 HP in the description. Its spec table matches the T12 Pro's word for word,
            which suggests it was copied.</li>
            <li><strong>T12 With Handles speed:</strong> 0.8 to 8.5 mph in the specs, 0.5 to 5.5 mph in the description
            and the page address.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Two patterns are clear even with the gaps. The walking pads all stop at 4 mph, and the T12s at 7.5 mph, a
            brisk jog at best for most adults. And Merach is one of the few budget brands that gives a continuous motor
            rating at all: 1.25 HP on the W50 and W60 Plus, 0.75 HP on the T12 Pro. The continuous figure is the one that
            matters for long walks, and 0.75 HP is on the low side for regular jogging.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-pads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Merach Walking Pads: W50 TrekPad vs W60 Plus vs W50 Lite</h2>
<p>
            Merach's walking pads are its strongest products. All three are walking-only pads with powered incline, a
            remote or app, and no handrail, aimed at under-desk walking and incline sessions at home.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">W50 TrekPad: the one with the clearest figures</h3>
          <p>
            The W50 TrekPad has the most consistent capacity of any Merach: 400 lb on Merach's page and in its Amazon
            listing title, and it is the incline pick in our guide to the <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads with incline</a>.
            Its 41.3-inch belt is a little longer than the 35 to 40 inches common on flat pads, which helps a longer
            stride on a slope, and the 1.25 HP continuous rating is high for a pad. At 6.1 inches tall and 69.2 lb it is
            bulkier than the slimmest pads; it slides under a bed more easily than it lifts into a cupboard. On
            21 November 2026 Merach showed it as clearance and sold out, so Amazon is the likelier source.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">W60 Plus: the in-stock alternative</h3>
          <p>
            On paper the W60 Plus is a W50 with a refreshed body: the same 16.5 by 41.3-inch belt, the same 0.6 to 4 mph
            and the same 1.25 HP continuous motor, at the same $299.99. Merach says it stores flat or upright, which suits
            a room where floor space under furniture is short. The catch is capacity. Merach's page gives 330 lb, while
            independent coverage gives 400 lb, so a heavier walker should choose the W50 if both are available, or
            confirm the W60 Plus figure with Merach in writing.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">W50 Lite: only if it is clearly cheaper</h3>
          <p>
            The W50 Lite trades the TrekPad's motor and capacity for a Bluetooth speaker and less weight: 350 lb and 10%
            incline in its Amazon title, and 0.75 HP continuous by independent figures. That is still a reasonable
            walking pad, but it is not sold on Merach's US store, so Merach's own 30-day returns do not cover it, and it
            usually makes sense only when it is noticeably cheaper than the W50 or W60 Plus.
          </p>
          <p>
            For how Merach's pads compare with UREVO, WalkingPad and the rest, see our <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads</a> guide. A pad that will
            carry a heavier walker every day deserves the most attention to capacity: buy on the lowest figure any
            source gives, not the highest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="t12-treadmills" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Merach T12 Treadmills: Which of the Four to Buy</h2>
<p>
            The T12 is Merach's compact folding treadmill, and all four versions share the same idea: a short deck,
            handrails and console, a top speed of 7.5 mph or less, and a frame that folds upright on wheels. They are
            bigger and heavier than the walking pads, at about 100 lb, but give you rails to hold and, on three of the
            four, 15% powered incline.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>T12 Foldable ($369.99, sold out):</strong> the value pick when in stock. 15% incline, 300 lb, a
            16.5 by 42.5-inch belt and a 2.5 HP peak motor with no continuous figure.</li>
            <li><strong>T12 Pro ($529.99):</strong> the one to buy now. The same speed and incline, 320 lb, a 0.75 HP
            continuous motor and a tablet holder. Ask Merach which of its two belt sizes is right.</li>
            <li><strong>T12 Aura ($599.99):</strong> a T12 with a 15.6-inch casting screen. Worth it only if you want
            the screen, and plan on 300 lb, the lower of its two capacity figures.</li>
            <li><strong>T12 With Handles ($429.99):</strong> no incline and conflicting speed figures. Merach markets it
            for seniors; check the top speed before you buy.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of the T12s is a running treadmill. A 42.5-inch belt is short for a jogging stride, and 7.5 mph is an
            8-minute mile, the pace at which many runners only warm up. For incline walking, though, 15% is steeper than
            most treadmills at this price offer. A 0.75 HP continuous motor working at 15% under a heavy user is where a
            budget treadmill is most likely to struggle, so keep sessions moderate and the belt maintained.
          </p>
          <p>
            For older walkers, the T12 With Handles is the model Merach sells for seniors, but it is not the only choice,
            and some walkers prefer a full-length rail on a longer deck. Our roundup of <a href="/best-treadmills-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the best treadmills on Amazon</a> compares folding treadmills at similar prices with clearer published figures.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Merach Warranty and Returns</h2>
<p>
            Merach's warranty page (checked November 2026) puts treadmills under its equipment warranty: one year on the
            "Structural Frame and Other Parts and Components", starting from delivery. It adds that "when you become a
            registered member, you can enjoy an additional one-year warranty", for "a total of 2 years". That is why the
            product pages say "2 Years Warranty" while the T12 Aura's description says "a 1-year warranty": both are
            true, depending on whether you register. Merach also links to a paid extended warranty program.
          </p>
          <p>
            The page does not give separate terms for the motor, which many <a href="/best-treadmill-brands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmill brands</a> cover for longer than the
            frame. Exclusions include normal wear on parts such as pulleys, unauthorised modification, damage from use in
            bad weather, noise caused by poor maintenance and damage from third-party products. Claims go through
            Merach's contact page with a proof of purchase, receipt or order number.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bought on Amazon?</h3>
          <p>
            The warranty page does not say whether an Amazon purchase qualifies for the registration year, and a
            walking-pad review site reports that the W50's Amazon listing gives one year. Register anyway, keep the Amazon
            order number, and ask Merach support to confirm the second year in writing. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> explains how to weigh frame, motor and parts cover.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Returns</h3>
          <p>
            Merach's refund policy gives 30 days, though it says both "within 30 days of delivery" and "within 30 days of
            purchase". Items must be undamaged, resellable, complete and in the original packaging. If the return is
            your choice, you pay the return shipping; if it is Merach's error, such as a defect or the wrong item,
            Merach pays. Report damage or defects within 24 hours with photos or video, or the return may be treated as
            a change of mind. The policy covers merachfit.com orders only; Amazon orders follow Amazon's returns. Keep
            the box: a 100 lb treadmill without its packaging is very hard to return.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="troubleshooting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Merach Treadmill Troubleshooting and Support</h2>
<p>
            Merach does not publish a public list of error codes that we could find, and codes vary between models, so
            the manual for your exact model is the only reliable guide to a particular code. Most faults on walking pads
            and compact treadmills, though, have the same causes. Unplug the machine before touching the belt, the
            motor cover or the underside.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">It will not start, or stops on its own</h3>
          <p>
            Check the safety key or magnetic clip is seated, plug straight into a wall outlet rather than an extension
            lead, and check the <a href="/how-to-turn-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>power switch</a>. On walking pads that use a remote, replace the remote's battery and re-pair
            it, following the manual. A pad that starts or stops by itself is a fault to report under warranty rather
            than fix at home; one independent comparison notes owner reports of exactly that on the W50 Lite.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The belt slips, skips or drifts</h3>
          <p>
            Merach describes the W50's belt as self-lubricating, which reduces upkeep but does not remove it. A belt that
            hesitates under each step usually needs tensioning; one that drifts needs centring with the rear roller
            bolts, a quarter-turn at a time. See our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt slipping</a> for the
            steps, and check your manual before adding any lubricant to a pad sold as self-lubricating.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Contacting Merach</h3>
          <p>
            Merach's US support (checked November 2026) is by email at info.us@merachfit.com and by phone on
            +1 (833) 529-6620, Monday to Friday, 8 AM to 8 PM Eastern. Its site has an assembly guides page. Have your
            order number, the SKU (such as MR-T25B2-US) and a short video of the fault ready; Merach's return policy
            already asks for photos or video for defects, and a video usually speeds up a parts replacement.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Recalls</h3>
          <p>
            We found no US Consumer Product Safety Commission recall notice for a Merach treadmill or walking pad when we
            searched in November 2026. Check cpsc.gov with your model number before buying second-hand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="good-brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is Merach a Good Treadmill Brand?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>For walking, yes: Merach is one of the better Amazon-era brands, with high-capacity incline pads,
            continuous motor ratings and a two-year warranty once you register. For running, no: nothing it sells goes
            faster than 7.5 mph.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            What Merach does well is publish more than most budget brands. A continuous motor rating, a stated belt size,
            a capacity and a warranty with written terms are more than many <a href="/best-walking-pads-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking pads on Amazon</a> offer. Its pads'
            41.3-inch belts and the T12's 15% incline are genuinely useful features at under $600. And a US phone line
            with stated hours is worth something when a 70 lb pad arrives damaged.
          </p>
          <p>
            What it does badly is consistency. The T12 Aura's page gives two capacities, two belts, two motors and two
            warranty lengths; the W60 Plus's capacity depends on which source you read; and the W50's incline appears as
            both 10% and 12%. None of that means the machines are poor, but it does mean you should buy on the lowest
            figure any source gives. Trustpilot reviews of Merach's store are mixed, with praise for value alongside
            complaints about slow return handling, so read the one-star reviews before relying on the average.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Who Merach suits, and who it does not</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Suits:</strong> desk walkers and incline walkers, including heavier walkers on the W50 TrekPad's
            400 lb rating.</li>
            <li><strong>Suits:</strong> small homes that need a treadmill to fold away, if a 7.5 mph ceiling is enough.</li>
            <li><strong>Does not suit:</strong> runners, or anyone training for a race, who need 10 mph or more and a
            55 to 60-inch belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Does not suit:</strong> buyers who want long motor cover; two years is the ceiling, and only
            after registering.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Merach vs UREVO, UMAY and LifeSmart, and Cheaper Alternatives</h2>
<p>
            Merach's closest competitors are the other brands that sell mainly online. Our guide to <a href="/urevo-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">UREVO treadmills</a> covers a wider range of
            walking pads, from flat budget pads to a folding running treadmill, with an 18-month warranty that comes with
            conditions of its own. Our <a href="/umay-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">UMAY treadmills</a> guide covers a smaller brand with a one-year warranty and figures that also need checking. LifeSmart is a
            different kind of purchase: its PowerTouch models are full-size, self-folding treadmills, mostly at $999 and
            up, with a one-year warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            Against those, Merach's case is the W50 TrekPad's 400 lb rating and continuous motor figure, and the T12's
            15% incline at under $600. If you do not need incline, a flat walking pad from any established brand will
            cost less, and if you do need to run, a Merach is the wrong machine at any price: look for a 55 to 60-inch
            belt and 10 mph or more.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cheaper than a Merach</h3>
          <p>
            Merach's cheapest current machines are its $299.99 pads. Below that you are buying a flat walking pad with no
            powered incline, usually a smaller motor and a shorter belt, and often only a one-year warranty. That can be
            the right call for slow desk walking: if you will walk at 2 to 3 mph on the flat while working, the W50's
            incline and 400 lb frame are features you would not use. Our roundups of walking pads and budget treadmills,
            linked below, list cheaper machines with published capacities, and you should check each one's continuous
            motor rating where it is given, because that is where cheap pads save money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Buying a Merach Treadmill? Avoid These 4 Mistakes</h2>
          <p>Merach machines can be good value for walking. These are the mistakes that make them a poor buy.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a Merach to run on</h3>
              <p>Every current Merach tops out at 7.5 mph or less, with belts of about 41 to 43 inches. If you plan to run, look for 10 mph and a 55 to 60-inch belt instead.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not registering for the second year</h3>
              <p>Merach’s warranty is one year as standard and two only for registered members. Register on Merach’s site as soon as the machine arrives, and keep the order number, especially if you bought on Amazon.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Mixing up the W50 and W50 Lite</h3>
              <p>They share a name but not a motor or capacity. Check the title and the SKU or model number: Merach lists the W50 TrekPad as MR-T25B2-US.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting the highest capacity figure</h3>
              <p>The W60 Plus appears as 330 lb and 400 lb, and the T12 Aura as 300 lb and 320 lb. Buy on the lower figure, or get the higher one confirmed in writing.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>Our verdict on the brand</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Merach makes good-value walking machines, not running treadmills.</strong> The
          NovaWalk W50 TrekPad, with its 400 lb rating and published 1.25 HP continuous motor, is the one to buy, and the
          W60 Plus is the in-stock alternative if you can live with Merach's own 330 lb figure. Of the T12s, the T12 Pro
          at $529.99 is the pick for 15% incline in a folding frame.
          </p>
          <p>
            Register for the second warranty year, keep the box for 30 days, and buy on the lowest figure any source
          gives. If you want to run, or want longer motor cover, look at <a href="/best-treadmills-on-amazon/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">the best treadmills on Amazon</a> or compare <a href="/urevo-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">UREVO</a> before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

